  itself.

  First question — this one gates everything else. The fork: whether the identity is [1missued by a server[0m or [1mminted on the
[0m  [1mclient[0m. That decides what "identify the actual person" can actually mean here.

  - [1mServer-issued[0m — a backend authenticates the credentials and returns a stable account id. This is the only option
    that genuinely identifies a person: it survives cleared browser storage, works across devices and browsers, and can
    be trusted for anything security-adjacent. Costs a real backend; today [38;5;153mlogin()[39m at [38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m is a stub that POSTs
    nowhere. Expensive to retrofit onto data keyed by a client id.
  - [1mClient-minted[0m — the browser generates and persists a pseudonymous id. Cheap, works today, no backend. But it
    identifies a [3mbrowser profile[0m, not a person: it dies with cleared storage, doesn't follow the user to another device,
    and is trivially forgeable, so it can't be trusted for authorization. Cheap to add, expensive to later re-key if
    you thought it meant "person."
  - [1mBoth, layered[0m — mint a client id now for continuity, adopt the server id as canonical once login is real, and
    reconcile the two. Buys an incremental path; costs the reconciliation logic and a period where two ids are in play.

  My recommendation depends on a fact I don't have, which is why I'm asking rather than proposing: if a backend is in
  the picture at all, server-issued is the right target, because "the actual person" and "persists" are only truthfully
  satisfiable there — and building the client-minted version first tends to spread a browser-scoped id through the code
  under a name that promises more than it delivers.
